ns and classified by party through a two-step process: a profile classifier followed by an activity classifier.", "quotes": [{"text": "To identify partisan users, we developed and validated a two-step approach for label propagation that classifies users as conservative or liberal by cross-indexing multiple metadata sources.", "page": 4}, {"text": "We processed this free-form text field using Open Street Map and the ArcGIS API, which both return latitude and longitude coordinates when a location is found.", "page": 6}]}, {"claim": "The party classifications were validated externally against election results and against voter registration records in five states and DC.", "quotes": [{"text": "we matched Twitter users with primary voter registration records available for five states (Ohio, New York, Florida, Arkansas, North Carolina) and Washington DC.", "page": 8}, {"text": "obtaining strong correlations of 0.802 for the United States and 0.815 for Canada", "page": 8}]}, {"claim": "External data include COVID case and death data, vaccination rates taken from one year after the tweet period, and U.S. voter registration records.", "quotes": [{"text": "We collected Twitter (X) data, COVID-19 case data from official government websites, vaccination rates from public health records, and U.S. voter registration records from state government portals.", "page": 4}, {"text": "We used officially reported vaccination rates from one year after our Twitter data collection period, as COVID-19 vaccines were only created and approved at the very end of our initial data collection.", "page": 5}]}], "key_findings": [{"claim": "In the U.S., Republican vote share correlates strongly with polarization on masks and vaccines but not on lockdowns. In Canada the associations are not statistically significant.", "quotes": [{"text": "We find strong, significant correlations in the US between Republican vote share and polarization around masks and vaccines, though not for lockdowns.", "page": 16}