n is measured only through semantic similarity, which may miss signals that embeddings do not capture.", "quotes": [{"text": "First, we measured partisan polarization only through the proxy of semantic similarity, which may in certain cases obscure signals not captured by semantic embedding representations.", "page": 24}]}, {"claim": "Aggregating tweets into user-level embeddings may obscure subtle rhetoric such as sarcasm or indirect disagreement.", "quotes": [{"text": "As a result, subtle rhetorical strategies such as sarcasm or indirect disagreement may be less apparent, while consistent linguistic cues, including negation, are preserved in the aggregated representation.", "page": 10}]}, {"claim": "Party classification relies on self-reported information, and Bloc Québécois supporters were excluded.", "quotes": [{"text": "During manual annotation of Twitter (X) profiles, we encountered few users who identified as Bloc Québécois supporters and excluded them from analysis.", "page": 24}, {"text": "Additionally, our classification into partisan groups relies on self-reported information, which may not be entirely accurate.", "page": 24}]}, {"claim": "Twitter users are more politically engaged and partisan than the general public, so the results reflect a vocal subset rather than the broader population.", "quotes": [{"text": "Twitter users are more politically engaged and often more partisan than the general population, which means our results reflect dynamics within a vocal subset of online political discourse.", "page": 24}]}, {"claim": "The analysis covers English-language tweets only, which may bias the Canadian results, especially for Quebec.", "quotes": [{"text": "Fourth, we restricted our analysis to English-language tweets, which in Canada means we primarily captured perspectives of either anglophones or bilingual francophones — a potential source of bias.", "page": 24}]}, {"claim": "Self-reported locations, VPNs and bots may introduce error, and bots could i